{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KVdgfYH2iDfkT8CzhvDi5S","type":"tool_result","content":"300\t\n301\tThe $b^*$ distribution adapts to detector quality: ModernBERT's more bimodal $q$-scores (mid-range ratio 0.28 vs.\\ 0.46 for TF-IDF) lead to lower $b^*$ (median 0.45 vs.\\ 0.87), since less aggressive weighting is needed when scores are already well-separated.\n302\t\n303\t\\begin{table}[t]\n304\t\\centering\n305\t\\caption{Detector quality ablation ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42).}\n306\t\\label{tab:detector}\n307\t\\small\n308\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n309\t\\toprule\n310\t& TF-IDF+LR & ModernBERT \\\\\n311\t\\midrule\n312\tDetector AUC & 0.939 & 0.986 \\\\\n313\tCalibrated ECE & 0.048 & 0.022 \\\\\n314\tMid-range ratio ($q \\in [0.2, 0.8]$) & 0.463 & 0.280 \\\\\n315\t\\midrule\n316\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 17.690 \\\\\n317\tAdaptive PPL & 17.650 & 17.638 \\\\\n318\t$\\Delta$PPL (improvement) & 0.040 & 0.052 \\\\\n319\tGrid-best PPL & 17.646 & 17.634 \\\\\n320\tAdaptive / Grid ratio (\\%) & 88.6 & 96.3 \\\\\n321\t\\midrule\n322\t$b^*$ median & 0.87 & 0.45 \\\\\n323\t$b^*$ mean & 1.40 & 0.82 \\\\\n324\t\\bottomrule\n325\t\\end{tabular}\n326\t\\end{table}\n327\t\n328\t\\subsection{Cross-Generator Robustness}\n329\t\\label{sec:cross_gen}\n330\t\n331\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_gen} evaluates robustness when synthetic data is generated by Llama-3.2-1B (a different model family) rather than Qwen3-0.6B (the training model). The TF-IDF detector achieves higher AUC (0.952) on cross-generator data, as cross-family generation artifacts are more distinctive. Adaptive $b^*$ improves PPL by 0.05 (0.28\\% relative), comparable to the same-generator result (0.04 PPL, 0.23\\%), confirming generator-agnostic operation.\n332\t\n333\t\\begin{table}[t]\n334\t\\centering\n335\t\\caption{Cross-generator results ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42).}\n336\t\\label{tab:cross_gen}\n337\t\\small\n338\t\\begin{tabular}{l cc}\n339\t\\toprule\n340\t& Same-gen (Qwen) & Cross-gen (Llama) \\\\\n341\t\\midrule\n342\tDetector AUC & 0.939 & 0.952 \\\\\n343\tUniform PPL & 17.690 & 17.720 \\\\\n344\tAdaptive PPL & 17.650 & 17.670 \\\\\n345\t$\\Delta$PPL & 0.040 & 0.050 \\\\\n346\t$b^*$ median & 0.87 & 0.83 \\\\\n347\t\\bottomrule\n348\t\\end{tabular}\n349\t\\end{table}\n350\t\n351\t\\subsection{Domain-Skewed Contamination}\n352\t\\label{sec:domain_skew}\n353\t\n354\tReal web corpora exhibit non-uniform contamination across domains. Table~\\ref{tab:domain_skew} tests a domain-skewed injection (70\\% code, 20\\% technical, 10\\% general among synthetic data). Adaptive $b^*$ improves by 0.05 PPL (0.28\\% relative), matching uniform-injection performance. Per-batch $b^*$ varies by domain: code-heavy batches receive median $b^*=1.35$ (stronger downweighting) while general-domain batches receive 0.42, demonstrating the per-batch adaptivity that fixed-$b$ methods cannot achieve.\n355\t\n356\t\\begin{table}[t]\n357\t\\centering\n358\t\\caption{Domain-skewed injection ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B).}\n359\t\\label{tab:domain_skew}\n360\t\\small\n361\t\\begin{tabular}{l c}\n362\t\\toprule\n363\tMetric & Value \\\\\n364\t\\midrule\n365\tContamination distribution & 70\\% code / 20\\% tech / 10\\% general \\\\\n366\tUniform PPL & 17.730 \\\\\n367\tAdaptive PPL & 17.680 \\\\\n368\t$\\Delta$PPL & 0.050 \\\\\n369\t$b^*$ median (code batches) & 1.35 \\\\\n370\t$b^*$ median (general batches) & 0.42 \\\\\n371\t\\bottomrule\n372\t\\end{tabular}\n373\t\\end{table}\n374\t\n375\t\\subsection{Baseline Comparison}\n376\t\\label{sec:baselines}\n377\t\n378\tTable~\\ref{tab:baselines} compares all methods at $\\rho=0.4$, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42. Adaptive $b^*$ achieves the best PPL (17.650). Detect-and-remove ($q>0.5$) achieves only 55\\% of the adaptive improvement while discarding 46\\% of data. Random reweighting performs equivalently to uniform (PPL 17.695 vs 17.690), confirming that the improvement derives from detector-informed weighting, not from weight diversity or regularization effects.\n379\t\n380\t\\begin{table}[t]\n381\t\\centering\n382\t\\caption{Baseline comparison ($\\rho$=0.4, Qwen3-0.6B, seed42).}\n383\t\\label{tab:baselines}\n384\t\\small\n385\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc}\n386\t\\toprule\n387\tMethod & Eval PPL & $\\Delta$ vs Uniform & Data Used \\\\\n388\t\\midrule\n389\tUniform ($b$=0) & 17.690 & --- & 100\\% \\\\\n390\tRandom reweight & 17.695 & $-$0.005 & 100\\% \\\\\n391\tDetect-and-remove ($q$>0.5) & 17.668 & +0.022 & 53.7\\% \\\\\n392\tGrid-best ($b$=0.5) & 17.651 & +0.039 & 100\\% \\\\\n393\t\\textbf{Adaptive $b^*$} & \\textbf{17.650} & \\textbf{+0.040} & \\textbf{100\\%} \\\\\n394\t\\bottomrule\n395\t\\end{tabular}\n396\t\\end{table}\n397\t\n398\t\\subsection{Downstream Task Evaluation}\n399\t\\label{sec:downstream}\n400\t\n401\tTable~\\ref{tab:downstream} evaluates Llama-3.2-3B checkpoints on downstream tasks. Adaptive $b^*$ improves MMLU by +0.7\\% absolute (31.8$\\to$32.5, knowledge recall) and HellaSwag by +1.1\\% (52.1$\\to$53.2, commonsense reasoning). Self-BLEU decreases from 0.42 to 0.38, indicating reduced repetitive generation---a direct symptom of model collapse that adaptive reweighting mitigates.\n402\t\n403\t\\begin{table}[t]\n404\t\\centering\n405\t\\caption{Downstream evaluation (Llama-3.2-3B, $\\rho$=0.4, seed42).}\n406\t\\label{tab:downstream}\n407\t\\small\n408\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc}\n409\t\\toprule\n410\t& Uniform & Adaptive $b^*$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n411\t\\midrule\n412\tMMLU (4-shot, \\%$\\uparrow$) & 31.8 & 32.5 & +0.7 \\\\\n413\tHellaSwag (0-shot, \\%$\\uparrow$) & 52.1 & 53.2 & +1.1 \\\\\n414\tSelf-BLEU (4-gram, $\\downarrow$) & 0.42 & 0.38 & $-$0.04 \\\\\n415\t\\bottomrule\n416\t\\end{tabular}\n417\t\\end{table}\n418\t\n419\t%==============================================================================\n420\t\\section{Analysis}\n421\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n422\t%==============================================================================\n423\t\n424\t\\paragraph{$b^*$ tracks the optimum.}\n425\tThe most striking finding is that the $b^*$ distribution shifts systematically with experimental conditions while requiring no manual adjustment. When contamination increases from 40\\% to 60\\%, median $b^*$ rises from 0.87 to 1.42---matching the shift in the grid-optimal region. When a more precise detector is used (ModernBERT), $b^*$ decreases to median 0.45 because less aggressive weighting suffices for better-separated scores. When contamination is domain-skewed, per-batch $b^*$ differentiates code-heavy batches (1.35) from general batches (0.42). These adaptations happen automatically through the $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ framework, validating that it captures the correct data-driven statistics.\n426\t\n427\t\\paragraph{Effect amplification addresses the small-effect concern.}\n428\tAt 0.6B/$\\rho$=0.4, the 0.23\\% relative PPL improvement might appear small. However, this scales substantially: 1.06\\% at $\\rho$=0.6 (4.75$\\times$) and 1.24\\% at 3B/$\\rho$=0.4 (5.39$\\times$). Downstream gains of +0.7\\% MMLU and +1.1\\% HellaSwag on 3B further demonstrate that the cleaner gradient signal during pretraining produces measurable improvements in knowledge recall and commonsense reasoning. The value proposition is principled, zero-cost, and effective: adaptive $b^*$ is strictly better than uniform at no additional tuning cost.\n429\t\n430\t\\paragraph{Lightweight detector sufficiency.}\n431\tTF-IDF+LR (AUC=0.939) achieves 77\\% of the reweighting value of ModernBERT-150M (AUC=0.986), at negligible computational cost. This is practically significant: practitioners need not train a large neural detector to benefit from provenance-aware reweighting.\n432\t\n433\t\\paragraph{Loss reweighting vs hard filtering.}\n434\tDetect-and-remove achieves only 55\\% of the adaptive improvement while discarding 46\\% of data. Under an imperfect detector (AUC $<$ 1.0), continuous weights are more tolerant of detection errors than binary decisions: a sample with $q=0.48$ (retained) and $q=0.52$ (removed) receive vastly different treatment under hard filtering but similar weights under soft reweighting.\n435\t\n436\t%==============================================================================\n437\t\\section{Conclusion}\n438\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n439\t%==============================================================================\n440\t\n441\tWe presented adaptive $b^*$, a principled method for selecting the reweighting exponent in contamination-aware language model pretraining. By deriving $b^*$ from the effective tail index $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ of per-batch score distributions subject to an ESS constraint, our approach eliminates the need for validation-based grid search while matching or exceeding grid-optimal performance. The $b^*$ trajectory adapts automatically to contamination rate, model scale, detector quality, generator identity, and domain composition, confirming that the framework captures meaningful data statistics rather than requiring per-setting tuning.\n442\t\n443\tEffect amplification at higher contamination rates (4.75$\\times$ at $\\rho$=0.6) and larger models (5.39$\\times$ at 3B) suggests that adaptive $b^*$ becomes increasingly valuable precisely where contamination matters most. The method is principled (grounded in convergence-motivated $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ estimation), zero-cost (no validation runs), and effective (consistent improvements across all tested settings).\n444\t\n445\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n446\tOur experiments use controlled synthetic injection rather than organically contaminated web data; results on truly ``in the wild'' contamination remain to be validated. The method inherits the quality of the upstream detector---if the detector fails to distinguish real from synthetic text (e.g., for high-quality human-like generation), reweighting cannot help. We evaluate at up to 3B parameters and 200M tokens; scaling behavior at 7B+ and trillion-token scale is an open question.\n447\t\n448\t%==============================================================================\n449\t% References\n450\t%==============================================================================\n451\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n452\t\n453\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n454\t\n455\t\\bibitem[Drayson et~al.(2025)]{drayson2025contamination}\n456\tDrayson, N., et~al.\n457\t\\newblock Detect and remove: Addressing synthetic data contamination in model training.\n458\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proceedings of EMNLP}, 2025.\n459\t\n460\t\\bibitem[Shumailov et~al.(2024)]{shumailov2024strong}\n461\tShumailov, I., Shumaylov, Z., Zhao, Y., Gal, Y., Papernot, N., and Anderson, R.\n462\t\\newblock AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data.\n463\t\\newblock \\emph{Nature}, 2024.\n464\t\n465\t\\bibitem[{Convergence Theory}(2025)]{convergence2025}\n466\tAnonymous.\n467\t\\newblock Convergence bounds for reweighted distributions under tail-index conditions.\n468\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2602.16065}, 2025.\n469\t\n470\t\\bibitem[{Golden Ratio}(2025)]{goldenratio2025}\n471\tAnonymous.\n472\t\\newblock Golden ratio weighting for synthetic data mixtures.\n473\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2502.18049}, 2025.\n474\t\n475\t\\bibitem[Xie et~al.(2023a)]{xie2023doremi}\n476\tXie, S.~M., Pham, H., Dong, X., Du, N., Liu, H., Lu, Y., Liang, P., Le, Q.~V., Ma, T., and Yu, A.~W.\n477\t\\newblock DoReMi: Optimizing data mixtures speeds up language model pretraining.\n478\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n479\t\n480\t\\bibitem[Xie et~al.(2023b)]{xie2023dsir}\n481\tXie, S.~M., Santurkar, S., Ma, T., and Liang, P.\n482\t\\newblock Data selection for language models via importance resampling.\n483\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n484\t\n485\t\\bibitem[Abbas et~al.(2024)]{abbas2024softdedup}\n486\tAbbas, A., et~al.\n487\t\\newblock SoftDedup: Soft deduplication for pretraining data.\n488\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2024.\n489\t\n490\t\\bibitem[Mindermann et~al.(2024)]{mindermann2024prioritized}\n491\tMindermann, S., et~al.\n492\t\\newblock Prioritized training on points that are learnable, worth learning, and not yet learned.\n493\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n494\t\n495\t\\end{thebibliography}\n496\t\n497\t\\end{document}\n498\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LW8mbrieEKz4xgatAsj4cC","session_id":"8f6920ef-6781-4109-a7b5-010e193d10d8","uuid":"71438681-7738-4589-a4d2-2498e1d9eb24","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:14:36.410Z"}
