{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QCJqZxf3gioCHSJh8ZMP2p","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n11\t\\usepackage{algorithm}\n12\t\\usepackage{algorithmic}\n13\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n14\t\n15\t\\title{Adaptive Provenance Weighting for Contaminated Pretraining Corpora:\\\\A Diagnostic Framework and Practical Guidelines}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{Anonymous}\n18\t\\date{}\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{document}\n21\t\\maketitle\n22\t\n23\t% ============================================================\n24\t\\begin{abstract}\n25\tLarge-scale web corpora increasingly contain machine-generated text of unknown provenance and proportion, degrading language model pretraining.\n26\tDetect-and-reweight approaches assign instance weights $w_i = (1-q_i)^b$ based on detector scores $q_i$, but the exponent $b$ must be tuned per model via grid search over a validation set.\n27\tWe introduce \\textbf{adaptive $b^*$}, which derives $b$ online from per-batch contamination statistics $\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ subject to an effective sample size (ESS) constraint, requiring no validation set and no known contamination ratio.\n28\tOur experiments on FineWeb subsets (200M tokens, $\\rho \\in \\{0.4, 0.6\\}$) with Qwen3-0.6B and Llama-3.2-3B reveal three findings.\n29\t\\textbf{(1)} A lightweight TF-IDF + logistic regression detector (AUC = 0.939) achieves within 0.003 PPL of a 150M-parameter ModernBERT detector (AUC = 0.986), establishing that expensive detectors are unnecessary for provenance weighting.\n30\t\\textbf{(2)} The $\\hat{\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ framework, while producing $b^*$ values that systematically overshoot the grid-optimal $b$ by approximately $4\\times$, serves as a diagnostic tool whose $b^*$ trajectory exposes batch-level contamination structure.\n31\t\\textbf{(3)} Adaptive $b^*$ provides a zero-cost reasonable default (recovering 70\\% of grid-best improvement) in standard settings, though it degrades under cross-generator contamination and domain-skewed injection.\n32\tWe additionally provide the first systematic comparison of loss reweighting versus sampling importance resampling (SIR) under identical weighting formulas.\n33\t\\end{abstract}\n34\t\n35\t% ============================================================\n36\t\\section{Introduction}\n37\t\\label{sec:intro}\n38\t\n39\tThe proliferation of machine-generated text on the web poses a growing challenge for language model pretraining.\n40\tRecent estimates suggest 20--60\\% of web-crawled corpora may contain synthetic content from diverse generators, with the exact proportion and provenance unknown at training time.\n41\tTheoretical analyses demonstrate that even small amounts of synthetic contamination can degrade scaling laws and induce model collapse~\\citep{shumailov2024strong}.\n42\t\n43\tDrayson et al.\\ propose a detect-and-reweight framework: train a detector to produce per-instance contamination scores $q_i \\in [0,1]$, then weight each sample by $w_i = (1 - q_i)^b$ during training via Sampling Importance Resampling (SIR).\n44\tWhile effective, this approach requires tuning $b$ per model through validation-based grid search---different model scales need different $b$ values (e.g., $b=10$ for GPT-2/SmolLM $\\leq$360M versus $b=1$ for SmolLM-1.7B), and the procedure assumes access to a clean validation set.\n45\t\n46\tWe propose \\textbf{adaptive $b^*$}: an online method that derives the weighting exponent from per-batch statistics of the detector score distribution, subject to an effective sample size (ESS) constraint.\n47\tOur method requires neither a validation set nor knowledge of the contamination proportion, making it applicable to web-scale pretraining where these are unavailable.\n48\tWe implement provenance weighting via direct loss reweighting rather than SIR, providing continuous gradient-level control.\n49\t\n50\tOur investigation yields three contributions, ordered by strength of evidence:"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ary1Xz6F5GeW2mATGqp1cc","session_id":"8f6920ef-6781-4109-a7b5-010e193d10d8","uuid":"a9b3a451-bf1c-420c-83c9-4b544c17f289","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:15:15.991Z"}
