{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a03c56eccd2731164","tool_use_id":"toolu_01LW8mbrieEKz4xgatAsj4cC","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":111282,"tool_uses":23,"duration_ms":165628},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"7f2e61cd-bb16-4405-a607-81704771f85b","session_id":"8f6920ef-6781-4109-a7b5-010e193d10d8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Cy87iyubbav52nMH9DekSN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PCgzQkh94sbxtRpic8cRPw","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"provenance_weight_training","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260515T011902","overall_score":"Weak Reject (3/6)","report":"\n### R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n**Summary:** The paper proposes adaptive b*, a per-batch mechanism for selecting the reweighting exponent in the detect-and-reweight framework of Drayson et al. (EMNLP 2025), replacing grid search with an optimization over an \"effective tail index\" alpha_eff(b) subject to an ESS constraint. Experiments at 132M-200M tokens across two model scales (0.6B, 3B), two contamination rates (40%, 60%), two detectors, cross-generator, and domain-skewed settings show that adaptive b* matches ~88.6% of grid-best improvement at zero tuning cost. The experimental matrix is commendably broad, but the method's theoretical grounding is insufficiently formalized, effect sizes are at the noise floor for the primary setting, and key scaling results lack multi-seed support.\n\n**Strengths:**\n\n- S1: **Comprehensive experimental coverage for a single-mechanism paper.** The experiment matrix spans model scale (0.6B, 3B), contamination rate (40%, 60%), detector quality (TF-IDF AUC=0.939, ModernBERT AUC=0.986), cross-generator (Llama-to-Qwen), domain-skewed injection, n_min sensitivity (1/2/4/5), and informative baselines (detect-and-remove, random reweight). This is thorough for a paper proposing a single adaptive rule.\n\n- S2: **Well-designed controls and ablations.** The random reweight baseline (PPL 17.695 vs uniform 17.690) cleanly isolates the detector signal from any regularization or weight-diversity effect. The n_min ablation reveals a clean phase structure: over-aggressive (n_min=1) through optimal (n_min=2) to degenerate/uniform (n_min >= batch_size). The degenerate case (n_min=4 recovers exactly uniform PPL) is a good sanity check.\n\n- S3: **b* distribution analysis is the paper's strongest empirical contribution.** The demonstration that b* shifts systematically with contamination rate (median 0.87 at rho=0.4 to 1.42 at rho=0.6), adapts to detector quality (median 0.87 TF-IDF vs 0.45 ModernBERT), differentiates domain-skewed batches (code 1.35 vs general 0.42), and is invariant to model scale (0.6B median 0.87 vs 3B median 0.88) -- all without manual intervention -- is genuinely informative. The cross-seed CV of b* statistics is remarkably low (<=2.5%), confirming b* is a data-driven quantity.\n\n- S4: **Algorithm 1 is simple and reproducible.** The code (alpha_eff.py, pretrain_weighted.py) is clean. The 1000-point grid search + bisection refinement for find_optimal_b is straightforward. The WeightedTrainer implementation within HuggingFace Trainer is a practical contribution.\n\n- S5: **Honest positioning relative to Drayson et al.** The paper explicitly acknowledges adopting the same weighting formula w=(1-q)^b and frames the contribution as adaptive b* selection within Drayson's framework, rather than claiming a new reweighting paradigm.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n\n- W1 (Major): **alpha_eff(b) lacks a formal definition in the paper, yet is claimed as a \"theoretical framework\" (Contribution 1).** Section 3.2 states alpha_eff is estimated \"from the Pareto tail behavior of the weight distribution\" but provides no closed-form expression. However, inspecting the code reveals that alpha_eff(b) = sum((1-q)^{b+1}) / sum((1-q)^b), which is simply the weighted mean of (1-q) under the weight distribution -- NOT a Pareto tail index in any standard sense (Hill estimator, Pickands estimator, etc.). The name \"effective tail index\" and the motivation via the convergence bound d(P_t, P_0) <= t^{-min{p,alpha}} create an impression of formal theoretical grounding that does not exist. The connection between the convergence bound's alpha (a distributional property of the true data) and the algorithm's alpha_eff (a batch statistic of detector scores) is never established. This makes Contribution 1 unsubstantiated as stated. The algorithm may work well as an empirical heuristic, but calling it \"principled\" or \"theoretically motivated\" requires a formal bridge that is absent.\n\n- W2 (Major): **No statistical significance test for the primary claim (adaptive vs grid-best).** The paper reports a paired t-test for the secondary comparison b=0.3 vs b=0.5 (p=0.42), making the omission of any test for the headline comparison (adaptive vs grid-best) conspicuous. At rho=0.4, adaptive mean PPL is 17.651 vs grid-best 17.646 -- a difference of 0.005, within the seed std of 0.007-0.009. The \"aggregate ratio\" of 88.6% is a non-standard metric that masks substantial per-seed heterogeneity (seed42: 102.6%, seed123: 90.4%, seed456: 80.0%). A 20% shortfall on seed456 is not negligible. The paper should report mean +/- std of PPL improvement, a paired test, and confidence intervals.\n\n- W3 (Major): **Effect amplification results (rho=0.6, 3B) are single-seed, undermining the paper's primary defense against \"small effect size.\"** The paper's rhetorical strategy is sound -- showing that effects scale with contamination rate (4.75x at rho=0.6) and model size (5.39x at 3B). But these are precisely the results that need multi-seed validation, since they are the paper's main evidence that the method produces meaningful improvements. Single-seed results at these settings cannot distinguish signal from noise, especially when the primary setting (rho=0.4, 0.6B) already shows seed-level variance of comparable magnitude to the effect.\n\n- W4 (Major): **b* is computed on micro-batches of size 4, not effective batches of size 16.** The code shows find_optimal_b is called in compute_loss on each micro-batch (batch_size=4) before gradient accumulation. Estimating alpha_eff from 4 samples is statistically dubious -- this is not enough data to characterize any distributional property reliably. With n_min=2, the ESS dynamic range is [2, 4], meaning the method operates at its extreme boundary for the majority of batches (75%+ batches have ESS approximately 2.0, per D015). The paper does not discuss this issue or analyze whether b* should be computed on the accumulated batch of 16 samples.\n\n- W5 (Minor): **n_min sensitivity reveals a cliff, not a plateau.** The viable range is n_min in {1, 2} for batch_size=4. The paper frames this as \"not a sensitive hyperparameter\" but the reality is: n_min=1 works but is suboptimal, n_min=2 is the only good value, n_min=3 caused seed42 to fail completely (the original version required a code fix from n_min=3 to n_min=2, per D012), and n_min>=4 degenerates to uniform. For batch_size=4, there is exactly one working value. The paper does not discuss how n_min scales with batch size -- at batch_size=256, n_min=2 is an ESS constraint of 0.78%, which may be too loose.\n\n- W6 (Minor): **Table 1 is truncated.** The grid search covers b in {0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0} but Table 1 only shows b <= 0.5. The claim that \"PPL rises sharply for b > 0.5\" is not supported by shown data. The full b-PPL curve is needed to assess the asymmetric plateau claim.\n\n- W7 (Minor): **No SIR vs loss-reweighting ablation.** Section 3.4 argues loss reweighting is superior to Drayson's SIR approach (\"preserves continuous, fine-grained control at the gradient level, avoids the discretization inherent in resampling\"), but no experiment compares the two under matched conditions. This claim is unsupported.\n\n- W8 (Minor): **Missing grid search on 3B.** The paper claims adaptive \"matches or exceeds grid-best\" but does not run grid search on 3B. The \"exceeds\" direction at 3B (ratio estimated at 114.3% in the imagined experiments) is entirely unvalidated. Without 3B grid search data, the headline claim at 3B is unverifiable.\n\n- W9 (Minor): **Downstream results (MMLU +0.7%, HellaSwag +1.1%) are single-seed, single-setting, with no error bars or significance tests.** At 200M tokens on a 3B model, these benchmarks have substantial variance. Without multi-seed results, these numbers cannot be interpreted as statistically meaningful.\n\n- W10 (Minor): **The convergence theory reference (arXiv:2602.16065) uses an invalid arXiv ID format** (2602 implies a non-existent month). This is the foundational reference motivating the entire method. If unverifiable, the theoretical motivation chain is broken.\n\n**Questions for Authors:**\n\n- Q1: What is the exact closed-form expression for alpha_eff(b)? The code shows it is sum((1-q)^{b+1})/sum((1-q)^b) -- a weighted mean of (1-q), not a Pareto tail index. Why is it called an \"effective tail index\"? What is the formal connection to the convergence bound's alpha?\n\n- Q2: Why is b* computed on micro-batches of 4 rather than accumulated batches of 16? Have you analyzed the estimation variance of alpha_eff from 4 samples? Would computing b* on the accumulated batch produce better or more stable results?\n\n- Q3: How does the method behave at realistic pretraining batch sizes (e.g., 256, 1024)? Does n_min=2 remain reasonable, or does it need to scale with batch size?\n\n- Q4: Can you provide a paired t-test (or bootstrap CI) for adaptive vs grid-best across the 3 seeds at rho=0.4? The current \"aggregate ratio\" metric obscures whether the difference is statistically meaningful.\n\n- Q5: Why are the amplification results (rho=0.6, 3B, downstream) all single-seed? These are the paper's strongest evidence against the \"small effect size\" concern, yet they have the weakest statistical support.\n\n- Q6: The decision log (D012) reveals that the original n_min=3 caused seed42 to fail completely, requiring a fix to n_min=2. How do you reconcile this with the claim that n_min is \"not a sensitive hyperparameter\"?\n\n- Q7: What is the computational overhead of find_optimal_b per batch? The code performs a 1001-point grid search + 50-iteration bisection for each micro-batch of 4 samples. At 6103 training steps x 4 gradient accumulation steps = ~24,412 calls, what fraction of training time is spent on b* optimization?\n\n**Minor Issues:**\n\n- The bibliography has only 8 references, which is thin for a paper touching model collapse, importance weighting, tail-index estimation, data curation, and contamination detection. Notable absences: importance sampling literature (Owen, Kahn-Marshall), tail-index estimation (Hill, Pickands), data curation (DataComp, RedPajama, FineWeb).\n- The \"aggregate ratio\" is a bespoke metric not used elsewhere in the literature. Standard reporting (mean +/- std of PPL improvement, significance tests) would be more interpretable.\n- The paper mixes \"imagined\" and real experimental results without clear demarcation. Per the project artifacts, only the rho=0.4 / Qwen3-0.6B / 3-seed experiments are actually completed; the rho=0.6, 3B, cross-generator, domain-skewed, and downstream results are all imagined/simulated.\n- The tau parameter (alpha_eff target threshold) is set to 1 - contamination_ratio * 0.5, which implicitly uses the contamination ratio -- somewhat undermining the \"unknown contamination proportion\" claim. Though in practice the code defaults tau based on the user-provided contamination_ratio argument.\n\n**Score:** 3/6\n**Confidence:** 4/5\n**Justification:** The paper proposes a practical and potentially useful adaptive mechanism for a single hyperparameter in an existing framework, supported by a genuinely comprehensive experimental matrix. However, three issues prevent acceptance at a top venue: (1) the \"theoretical framework\" contribution is overclaimed -- alpha_eff is a heuristic batch statistic dressed up as a formal tail index with no proven connection to the cited convergence theory; (2) statistical evidence for the central claim is insufficient, with no significance tests for the primary comparison and single-seed support for the amplified-effect results that are supposed to address the small effect size concern; (3) the method operates at extreme statistical boundaries (4-sample micro-batches, ESS dynamic range [2,4], single viable n_min value), raising questions about generalization to realistic training settings. The b*-tracks-optimum empirical pattern is the paper's strongest finding, but the theoretical wrapping does not support the claims made.\n","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":689,"cache_read_input_tokens":110072,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":689,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LW8mbrieEKz4xgatAsj4cC","session_id":"8f6920ef-6781-4109-a7b5-010e193d10d8","uuid":"ffea8ac4-c9f2-477a-b972-9fea381632e9"}
