{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LW8mbrieEKz4xgatAsj4cC","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the R1 review:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Key findings from the review:**\n\nThe paper proposes adaptive b* to replace grid search over the exponent in w=(1-q)^b (Drayson et al.'s framework). The experimental matrix is genuinely comprehensive for a single-mechanism paper.\n\n**Three major weaknesses drove the score:**\n\n1. **alpha_eff(b) is a heuristic, not a formal tail index.** The code reveals alpha_eff(b) = sum((1-q)^{b+1})/sum((1-q)^b) -- a weighted mean of (1-q), not a Pareto tail index. The \"theoretical framework\" contribution (Contribution 1) is unsubstantiated. The connection to the convergence bound is never formalized.\n\n2. **No statistical significance test for the primary claim.** Adaptive vs grid-best differs by 0.005 PPL (within seed std of 0.007-0.009). The bespoke \"aggregate ratio\" metric masks per-seed variance of 80%-103%. A paired t-test is reported for the secondary comparison (b=0.3 vs b=0.5) but conspicuously absent for the headline claim.\n\n3. **Amplification results are single-seed.** The rho=0.6, 3B, and downstream results -- the paper's primary defense against \"small effect size\" -- all lack multi-seed support. These are precisely the settings that need the strongest statistical evidence.\n\n**Additional concerns:** b* computed on 4-sample micro-batches (statistically dubious tail estimation), n_min sensitivity is a cliff not a plateau (exactly one working value at batch_size=4), missing 3B grid search, missing SIR vs loss-reweighting ablation, truncated Table 1, thin bibliography, and an unverifiable convergence theory reference.\n\n**What works well:** The b*-tracks-optimum empirical pattern is convincing and useful. The random reweight baseline cleanly isolates the detector signal. The code is clean and reproducible. Honest positioning relative to Drayson et al.\n\nReview saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_10/projects/provenance_weight_training/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T011902/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a03c56eccd2731164 (use SendMessage with to: 'a03c56eccd2731164' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 114952\ntool_uses: 23\nduration_ms: 185745</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f6920ef-6781-4109-a7b5-010e193d10d8","uuid":"31443b76-01fc-40d4-987a-e4f7d3ff9618","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:17:03.233Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Review the paper described below independently. Do NOT consult other reviewers. Use a 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept. Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Adaptive Contamination-Aware Reweighting for Pretraining on Mixed Real-Synthetic Corpora\n\n**Core idea:** The paper proposes \"adaptive b*\", replacing the fixed exponent b in the weighting function w=(1-q)^b (from Drayson et al. 2025) with a per-batch optimized value. b* is derived by maximizing an effective power-law index α_eff(b) subject to an ESS (effective sample size) constraint ESS(b) ≥ n_min. The sole hyperparameter is n_min=2. This eliminates grid search over b values, reducing tuning cost from 6N to 1 run.\n\n**Experiments (132M–200M tokens):**\n- Extended grid search (b=0.1–5.0, 3 seeds): PPL landscape is flat in b=0.1–0.5 (range 0.014 PPL). Grid-best b=0.3.\n- Adaptive b* vs grid-best: aggregate ratio 85.8% (per-seed: 95.2%, 87.0%, 78.4%). Gap 0.002–0.011 PPL.\n- Method comparison: detect-and-remove (hard filter q>0.5) yields only 0.005 PPL improvement; random reweighting degrades by 0.005. Adaptive gets -0.040 PPL.\n- Effect amplification at ρ=0.6: improvement doubles (0.04→0.08), adaptive/grid ratio stable (88.9%).\n- Cross-model (Llama-3.2-3B): 2× amplification, b* distribution stable (median 0.85 vs 0.88).\n- Detector quality ablation: TF-IDF+LR (AUC 0.939) vs ModernBERT (AUC 0.986) → only 0.005 PPL difference. \"Lightweight detector sufficiency.\"\n- n_min sensitivity: n_min∈{1,2} stable, n_min≥3 degrades, n_min≥|B| → uniform. Graceful degradation.\n- Cross-generator (Llama-3.2-1B generates, Qwen trains): ratio drops to 75%, AUC drops to 0.925.\n- Domain-skewed contamination: ratio drops to 50%.\n- Downstream (MMLU, HellaSwag on 3B): not statistically significant at 200M tokens.\n\n**Tables in paper:** 9 tables covering all above experiments.\n\n**Experimental design context (from review_context.yaml):**\nThe imagined experiments list includes all the above experiments. The paper covers them comprehensively. Additional context: the narrative guidance says \"adaptive matches grid-best within seed variance, eliminating grid search (6N→1)\" is the primary claim. Secondary claim is \"comparable performance at zero tuning cost.\" Downstream improvements are inconsistent and relegated to supplementary. Cross-generator and domain-skewed are known weak points.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the α_eff(b) + ESS framework well-motivated? Is the optimization problem well-posed? Are there hidden assumptions? Is the connection to power-law theory rigorous or hand-wavy?\n\n2. **Experimental design**: Are controls adequate? Are baselines fair? Is the evaluation metric (PPL only) sufficient? Is the scale (132M–200M tokens, 0.6B–3B params) adequate for the claims made?\n\n3. **Statistical rigor**: 3 seeds for primary experiments, single seed for many ablations. Is cross-seed variance properly characterized? Are the claimed improvements statistically significant?\n\n4. **Reproducibility**: Algorithm 1 is provided. Are implementation details sufficient?\n\n5. **Effect size concerns**: Absolute improvements are 0.04–0.08 PPL. Is this meaningful? The paper argues the value is in zero-cost matching of grid search, not absolute gains.\n\n6. **Ablation completeness**: n_min, detector quality, contamination ratio, model scale, cross-generator, domain-skewed — comprehensive?\n\n7. **Key weaknesses in experiments**: domain-skewed (50% ratio), cross-generator (75%), no significant downstream gains, tiny scale.\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n```\n### R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n**Summary:** (2-3 sentences)\n\n**Strengths:**\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- ...\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- ...\n\n**Questions for Authors:**\n- Q1: ...\n- ...\n\n**Minor Issues:**\n- ...\n\n**Score:** X/6\n**Confidence:** X/5\n**Justification:** (2-3 sentences explaining score)\n```\n","agentId":"a03c56eccd2731164","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the R1 review:\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Key findings from the review:**\n\nThe paper proposes adaptive b* to replace grid search over the exponent in w=(1-q)^b (Drayson et al.'s framework). The experimental matrix is genuinely comprehensive for a single-mechanism paper.\n\n**Three major weaknesses drove the score:**\n\n1. **alpha_eff(b) is a heuristic, not a formal tail index.** The code reveals alpha_eff(b) = sum((1-q)^{b+1})/sum((1-q)^b) -- a weighted mean of (1-q), not a Pareto tail index. The \"theoretical framework\" contribution (Contribution 1) is unsubstantiated. The connection to the convergence bound is never formalized.\n\n2. **No statistical significance test for the primary claim.** Adaptive vs grid-best differs by 0.005 PPL (within seed std of 0.007-0.009). The bespoke \"aggregate ratio\" metric masks per-seed variance of 80%-103%. A paired t-test is reported for the secondary comparison (b=0.3 vs b=0.5) but conspicuously absent for the headline claim.\n\n3. **Amplification results are single-seed.** The rho=0.6, 3B, and downstream results -- the paper's primary defense against \"small effect size\" -- all lack multi-seed support. These are precisely the settings that need the strongest statistical evidence.\n\n**Additional concerns:** b* computed on 4-sample micro-batches (statistically dubious tail estimation), n_min sensitivity is a cliff not a plateau (exactly one working value at batch_size=4), missing 3B grid search, missing SIR vs loss-reweighting ablation, truncated Table 1, thin bibliography, and an unverifiable convergence theory reference.\n\n**What works well:** The b*-tracks-optimum empirical pattern is convincing and useful. The random reweight baseline cleanly isolates the detector signal. The code is clean and reproducible. Honest positioning relative to Drayson et al.\n\nReview saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_10/projects/provenance_weight_training/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T011902/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":185745,"totalTokens":114952,"totalToolUseCount":23,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3618,"cache_read_input_tokens":110761,"output_tokens":572,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3618},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":572,"cache_read_input_tokens":110761,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3618,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3618,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":13}}}
